{"id": "FT-R3", "version": 2, "type": "requirement", "subject": "design", "epistemic": "committed", "text": "Within the pair budget, candidate pairs are ranked by relevance to the declared question or target (e.g. strength of association, missingness linked to the target), not taken in column order; near-duplicate and near-constant columns don't consume the budget; the report states the ranking rule and what was excluded and why", "rationale": "Accepted by Zach 2026-11-01 16:46 CT (\"pretty much correct\"); data-quality checks come first", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "DG-RC@1"}, {"rel": "supersedes", "to": "FT-R3@1"}], "anchors": [{"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening.md", "sha256": "2b72ad6573419840163bae048f099578592b43838f3f63e43fd9d8f516bbbab8", "locator": "q:FTR2, R3, and R4 are all pretty much correct.", "for": "rationale"}]}
{"id": "FT-R4", "version": 2, "type": "requirement", "subject": "acceptance", "epistemic": "committed", "text": "A slice is judged useful by docs/acceptance.md: on a table it was not developed against, the report alone lets an analyst learn something consequential the raw table did not make obvious, without claiming more than it knows, and can serve as that table's standalone documentation. Builders check their own planted-problem tables and list found/missed in the PR", "rationale": "Accepted by Zach 2026-11-01 16:46 CT (\"pretty much correct\"); data-quality checks come first", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "P0@2"}, {"rel": "serves", "to": "DG-A1@1"}, {"rel": "supersedes", "to": "FT-R4@1"}], "anchors": [{"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening.md", "sha256": "2b72ad6573419840163bae048f099578592b43838f3f63e43fd9d8f516bbbab8", "locator": "q:FTR2, R3, and R4 are all pretty much correct.", "for": "rationale"}]}
{"id": "FT-R5", "version": 1, "type": "requirement", "subject": "design", "epistemic": "committed", "text": "The report opens with a dataset-level summary: rows and columns; complete rows (no missing cell) and complete columns (no missing value), as counts and shares; exact duplicate rows; duplicated or lockstep columns; columns by inferred type; constant and near-constant columns; and overall missing cells. Each figure links to the findings it supports", "rationale": "Stated by Zach 2026-11-01 16:46 CT", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "P0@2"}, {"rel": "serves", "to": "DG-A1@1"}], "anchors": [{"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening.md", "sha256": "2b72ad6573419840163bae048f099578592b43838f3f63e43fd9d8f516bbbab8", "locator": "q:How many rows were complete? How many columns were complete? How many columns were duplicated? How many columns of various types are there?", "for": "rationale"}]}
{"id": "FT-R6", "version": 1, "type": "requirement", "subject": "design", "epistemic": "committed", "text": "The report is readable by a person first: it leads with what the data cannot tell and with usefulness, then findings grouped by kind (observations, interpretations, hypotheses, suggested actions, open questions) as one plain sentence each with its evidence a click away, then relations, then a compact per-column dictionary, then provenance. Repetitive per-column findings (e.g. one type and one meaning question per column) are summarised, not listed one by one", "rationale": "Zach 2026-11-01 16:46 CT: slice 1's format is hard to read; he liked the structure of a reference report from an earlier prototype, described here in words (that report is not in this repository)", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "DG-A1@1"}, {"rel": "serves", "to": "DG-RA@1"}], "anchors": [{"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening.md", "sha256": "2b72ad6573419840163bae048f099578592b43838f3f63e43fd9d8f516bbbab8", "locator": "q:The format is atrocious to read", "for": "rationale"}, {"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening.md", "sha256": "2b72ad6573419840163bae048f099578592b43838f3f63e43fd9d8f516bbbab8", "locator": "q:I liked the format of guidereport.html.", "for": "rationale"}]}
{"id": "DG-D5", "version": 1, "type": "decision", "status": "accepted", "decided_by": "AU-owner", "subject": "design", "text": "Build order: data-quality checks first (FT-R2 to FT-R5), with readability (FT-R6); then more advanced univariate analyses and visuals; then bivariate; then multivariate", "anchors": [{"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening.md", "sha256": "2b72ad6573419840163bae048f099578592b43838f3f63e43fd9d8f516bbbab8", "locator": "q:That is what I would be interested in first, is data quality checks.", "for": "rationale"}, {"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening.md", "sha256": "2b72ad6573419840163bae048f099578592b43838f3f63e43fd9d8f516bbbab8", "locator": "q:For next steps, I would prefer some more advanced univariate analyses and visuals", "for": "rationale"}]}
// 2026-11-01 16:55 CT: FT-R6@2 (view switch), FT-R7 (target analyses), FT-R8 (switchable charts), DG-D5@2.
{"id": "FT-R6", "version": 2, "type": "requirement", "subject": "design", "epistemic": "committed", "text": "The report is readable by a person first: it leads with what the data cannot tell and with usefulness, then findings grouped by kind (observations, interpretations, hypotheses, suggested actions, open questions) as one plain sentence each with its evidence a click away, then relations, then a compact per-column dictionary, then provenance. Repetitive per-column findings (e.g. one type and one meaning question per column) are summarised, not listed one by one. The HTML offers a one-click switch between the summarised view and the full per-item detail, self-contained (inline script or native expand/collapse, no external assets); the summarised view is the default, and both views derive from the same artifact", "rationale": "Zach 2026-11-01 16:46 CT: slice 1's format is hard to read; he liked the structure of a reference report from an earlier prototype, described here in words (that report is not in this repository)", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "DG-A1@1"}, {"rel": "serves", "to": "DG-RA@1"}, {"rel": "supersedes", "to": "FT-R6@1"}], "anchors": [{"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening.md", "sha256": "2b72ad6573419840163bae048f099578592b43838f3f63e43fd9d8f516bbbab8", "locator": "q:The format is atrocious to read", "for": "rationale"}, {"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening.md", "sha256": "2b72ad6573419840163bae048f099578592b43838f3f63e43fd9d8f516bbbab8", "locator": "q:I liked the format of guidereport.html.", "for": "rationale"}, {"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening-2.md", "sha256": "4f7785545588bc80069709a7faf775df9106ca5cb5b593ef48382c403bb7be5a", "locator": "q:Is it possible to have that summarization piece clickable in the HTML", "for": "rationale"}]}
{"id": "FT-R7", "version": 1, "type": "requirement", "subject": "design", "epistemic": "committed", "text": "When a target (dependent variable) is declared, the report compares the target with each other column using light, type-appropriate measures of association or shared information (for example correlation, correlation ratio, Cramer's V, mutual information, target rates by group or bin), not a fitted model per column; it ranks columns by that relationship and labels every measure as descriptive, not causal. Target-versus-pairs of columns comes later. Model-based comparisons, if any, are opt-in", "rationale": "Stated by Zach 2026-11-01 16:55 CT as an addendum to DG-D5. The measures listed are examples; a spec chooses them", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "P0@2"}, {"rel": "serves", "to": "DG-RC@1"}], "anchors": [{"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening-2.md", "sha256": "4f7785545588bc80069709a7faf775df9106ca5cb5b593ef48382c403bb7be5a", "locator": "q:I would also like target-based analyses.", "for": "rationale"}, {"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening-2.md", "sha256": "4f7785545588bc80069709a7faf775df9106ca5cb5b593ef48382c403bb7be5a", "locator": "q:I don't want every bivariate comparison to run like a logistic regression", "for": "rationale"}, {"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening-2.md", "sha256": "4f7785545588bc80069709a7faf775df9106ca5cb5b593ef48382c403bb7be5a", "locator": "q:and also eventually we'll want target versus bivariate.", "for": "rationale"}]}
{"id": "FT-R8", "version": 1, "type": "requirement", "subject": "design", "epistemic": "committed", "text": "Where it saves room, charts are switchable in the self-contained HTML: the reader picks the variable on the x axis and on the y axis (or the column shown) from controls, instead of the report printing one chart per combination. The choices offered stay within what the question and the budget selected (DG-RC), and the static Markdown keeps a non-interactive fallback", "rationale": "Stated by Zach 2026-11-01 16:55 CT, after a Power BI report he configured with click-to-change axes", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "DG-RC@1"}, {"rel": "serves", "to": "DG-A1@1"}], "anchors": [{"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening-2.md", "sha256": "4f7785545588bc80069709a7faf775df9106ca5cb5b593ef48382c403bb7be5a", "locator": "q:could be clicked to change to other variables", "for": "rationale"}]}
{"id": "DG-D5", "version": 2, "type": "decision", "status": "accepted", "decided_by": "AU-owner", "subject": "design", "text": "Build order: data-quality checks first (FT-R2 to FT-R5), with readability (FT-R6); then more advanced univariate analyses and visuals; then bivariate and multivariate, including target-based analyses (FT-R7: target versus each column, later target versus pairs) and switchable charts (FT-R8)", "anchors": [{"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening.md", "sha256": "2b72ad6573419840163bae048f099578592b43838f3f63e43fd9d8f516bbbab8", "locator": "q:That is what I would be interested in first, is data quality checks.", "for": "rationale"}, {"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening.md", "sha256": "2b72ad6573419840163bae048f099578592b43838f3f63e43fd9d8f516bbbab8", "locator": "q:For next steps, I would prefer some more advanced univariate analyses and visuals", "for": "rationale"}, {"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening-2.md", "sha256": "4f7785545588bc80069709a7faf775df9106ca5cb5b593ef48382c403bb7be5a", "locator": "q:Addendum to DGD5.", "for": "rationale"}], "relations": [{"rel": "supersedes", "to": "DG-D5@1"}]}
{"id": "FT-DQ-R1", "version": 1, "type": "requirement", "subject": "design", "epistemic": "provisional", "text": "Implement FT-R2@2 through FT-R6@2 using the transparent diagnostic thresholds, shared ranking budget and artifact-derived report refinements in specs/002-data-quality and the data-quality intent note. These algorithmic readings are proposed for Zach to accept; they do not change the accepted requirements.", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "P0@2"}, {"rel": "implements", "to": "FT-R2@2"}, {"rel": "implements", "to": "FT-R3@2"}, {"rel": "implements", "to": "FT-R4@2"}, {"rel": "implements", "to": "FT-R5@1"}, {"rel": "implements", "to": "FT-R6@2"}]}
{"id": "FT-DQ-R1", "version": 2, "type": "requirement", "subject": "design", "epistemic": "provisional", "text": "Implement FT-R2@2 through FT-R6@2 using the transparent diagnostic thresholds, shared ranking budget and artifact-derived report refinements in specs/002-data-quality and the data-quality intent note. These algorithmic readings are proposed for Zach to accept; they do not change the accepted requirements.", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "P0@2"}, {"rel": "implements", "to": "FT-R2@2"}, {"rel": "implements", "to": "FT-R3@2"}, {"rel": "implements", "to": "FT-R4@2"}, {"rel": "implements", "to": "FT-R5@1"}, {"rel": "implements", "to": "FT-R6@2"}, {"rel": "supersedes", "to": "FT-DQ-R1@1"}, {"rel": "implemented_by", "to": "code:src.fathom.quality.quality_profile"}, {"rel": "implemented_by", "to": "code:src.fathom.profiler.profile"}, {"rel": "implemented_by", "to": "code:src.fathom.profiler.rank_pairs"}, {"rel": "implemented_by", "to": "code:src.fathom.models.validate_document"}, {"rel": "implemented_by", "to": "code:src.fathom.renderers.render_html"}, {"rel": "implemented_by", "to": "code:src.fathom.renderers.render_markdown"}], "anchors": [{"path": "specs/002-data-quality/spec.md", "sha256": "86f3ace794ef621aa8c721c8d032d32f55e794dea0b874f6ab40796d4de473c8", "locator": "q:An analyst receives findings rather than a collection of statistics", "for": "text"}]}
{"id": "FT-DQ-C1", "version": 1, "type": "claim", "tier": "candidate", "claim_category": "requirement_satisfaction", "text": "Slice 002 conforms to the tested FT-R2..FT-R6 scope: evidenced quality diagnostics, shared-budget relevance ranking, linked dataset summary and readable grouped/full artifact-derived reports. Builder found/missed results and known false alarms are disclosed; independent usefulness and owner acceptance are not claimed.", "relations": [{"rel": "implements", "to": "FT-R2@2"}, {"rel": "implements", "to": "FT-R3@2"}, {"rel": "implements", "to": "FT-R4@2"}, {"rel": "implements", "to": "FT-R5@1"}, {"rel": "implements", "to": "FT-R6@2"}, {"rel": "implements", "to": "FT-DQ-R1@2"}]}
{"id": "FT-DQ-V1", "version": 1, "type": "evaluation", "text": "Test slice-2 conformance and inspect reports against workshop, clean and later builder-holdout planted truth, plus native-browser view-switch checks.", "conditions": "Python 3.12.14; pandas 2.2.3, PyYAML 6.0.3, NumPy 2.5.4, pytest 9.2.0; core without SciPy and stats with SciPy 1.18.1; system Chromium through available Playwright. Synthetic fixtures only; diagnostic hash frozen before holdout creation.", "relations": [{"rel": "tests", "to": "FT-DQ-C1@1"}, {"rel": "produced", "to": "FT-DQ-E1@1"}, {"rel": "produced", "to": "FT-DQ-E2@1"}]}
{"id": "FT-DQ-E1", "version": 1, "type": "evidence", "tier": "candidate", "evidence_kind": "contract_conformance", "text": "100 tests pass with optional statistics available; core-only run has 99 passed and 1 optional-presence decoy skipped. Tests cover exact diagnostics/summary, violation/decoys, stronger-later/reordered/shared-budget ranking, screening limits, retained overrides, legacy artifacts, text escaping, CLI fresh-process bytes and saved-artifact views. Chromium confirms the summarized default, one-click disclosure of 44 grouped original workshop items, return to summary, unique IDs and resolving evidence links.", "method": "python -m pytest -q in core and stats environments; installed guide profile/render workflow; headless Chromium native-control verification.", "limits": "Machine-produced synthetic conformance evidence, not independent usefulness acceptance or a purpose outcome. See specs/002-data-quality/validation.md.", "relations": [{"rel": "supports", "to": "FT-DQ-C1@1"}, {"rel": "observed_on", "to": "code:src.fathom.quality.quality_profile"}, {"rel": "observed_on", "to": "code:src.fathom.profiler.rank_pairs"}, {"rel": "observed_on", "to": "code:src.fathom.renderers.render_html"}, {"rel": "observed_on", "to": "code:src.fathom.renderers.render_markdown"}, {"rel": "observed_on", "to": "code:tests.test_quality.test_workshop_report_finds_planted_families_and_clean_decoy"}, {"rel": "observed_on", "to": "code:tests.test_readability.test_summary_links_and_artifact_only_roundtrip"}, {"rel": "observed_on", "to": "code:tests.test_cli.test_cli_roundtrip_compare_validate_and_reproducibility"}]}
{"id": "FT-DQ-E2", "version": 1, "type": "evidence", "tier": "candidate", "evidence_kind": "behavioral_observation", "text": "Workshop (41x13) surfaces all planted families; clean (12x5) has no quality reviews. Later builder holdout (31x16) surfaces supported sentinel/dummy/near-copy/missingness/date-gap/skew/dominance/constant/duplicate patterns. It misses the intended monthly gap; unlisted 888 and test alice are only partially surfaced (skew/rarity, not sentinel/dummy diagnoses); a known-valid 999 triggers an unnecessary sentinel hypothesis/review. Rules were not tuned after holdout.", "method": "Builder compared generated HTML/Markdown findings/evidence against tests/fixtures/quality/planted.json, without relying only on dictionary statistics.", "limits": "Builder-authored truth and builder inspection are not Zach/Kate's unseen-table trial. Suspected meanings remain hypotheses, not confirmed defects.", "relations": [{"rel": "supports", "to": "FT-DQ-C1@1"}, {"rel": "observed_on", "to": "code:src.fathom.quality.quality_profile"}, {"rel": "observed_on", "to": "code:tests.test_quality.test_later_builder_holdout_report_evidence"}]}
{"id": "FT-DQ-V1", "version": 2, "type": "evaluation", "text": "Test slice-2 conformance and inspect reports against workshop, clean and later builder-holdout planted truth, plus native-browser view-switch checks.", "conditions": "Python 3.12.14; pandas 2.2.3, PyYAML 6.0.3, pytest 9.2.0. Final stats run: preconfigured SciPy 1.17.0, 101 passed. Dedicated core venv without usable SciPy: 100 passed, 1 skipped. Earlier clean stats run had SciPy 1.18.1 and 100 tests. System Chromium verifies native switch. Diagnostic freeze before first holdout, later shared-absence guard correction recorded explicitly.", "relations": [{"rel": "tests", "to": "FT-DQ-C1@1"}, {"rel": "produced", "to": "FT-DQ-E1@2"}, {"rel": "produced", "to": "FT-DQ-E2@2"}, {"rel": "supersedes", "to": "FT-DQ-V1@1"}]}
{"id": "FT-DQ-E1", "version": 2, "type": "evidence", "tier": "candidate", "evidence_kind": "contract_conformance", "text": "101 tests pass with optional statistics available; core-only run has 100 passed and 1 optional-presence decoy skipped. Tests cover exact diagnostics/summary, violation/decoys, stronger-later/reordered/shared-budget ranking, screening limits, retained overrides, legacy artifacts, text escaping, CLI fresh-process bytes and saved-artifact views. Chromium confirms the summarized default, one-click disclosure of 44 grouped original workshop items, return to summary, unique IDs and resolving evidence links. A shared-absence adversarial decoy verifies that a near-copy diagnosis/exclusion also requires jointly-present agreement.", "method": "python -m pytest -q in core and stats environments; installed guide profile/render workflow; headless Chromium native-control verification.", "limits": "Machine-produced synthetic conformance evidence, not independent usefulness acceptance or a purpose outcome. See specs/002-data-quality/validation.md.", "relations": [{"rel": "supports", "to": "FT-DQ-C1@1"}, {"rel": "observed_on", "to": "code:src.fathom.quality.quality_profile"}, {"rel": "observed_on", "to": "code:src.fathom.profiler.rank_pairs"}, {"rel": "observed_on", "to": "code:src.fathom.renderers.render_html"}, {"rel": "observed_on", "to": "code:src.fathom.renderers.render_markdown"}, {"rel": "observed_on", "to": "code:tests.test_quality.test_workshop_report_finds_planted_families_and_clean_decoy"}, {"rel": "observed_on", "to": "code:tests.test_readability.test_summary_links_and_artifact_only_roundtrip"}, {"rel": "observed_on", "to": "code:tests.test_cli.test_cli_roundtrip_compare_validate_and_reproducibility"}, {"rel": "supersedes", "to": "FT-DQ-E1@1"}, {"rel": "observed_on", "to": "code:tests.test_quality.test_shared_absence_is_not_near_copy_evidence"}]}
{"id": "FT-DQ-E2", "version": 2, "type": "evidence", "tier": "candidate", "evidence_kind": "behavioral_observation", "text": "Workshop (41x13) surfaces all planted families; clean (12x5) has no quality reviews. Later builder holdout (31x16) surfaces supported sentinel/dummy/near-copy/missingness/date-gap/skew/dominance/constant/duplicate patterns. It misses the intended monthly gap; unlisted 888 and test alice are only partially surfaced (skew/rarity, not sentinel/dummy diagnoses); a known-valid 999 triggers an unnecessary sentinel hypothesis/review. A separate later shared-absence audit decoy exposed a null-agreement false near-copy alarm; a generic jointly-present agreement guard corrected it. Both hashes/timing are recorded; original holdout misses were not tuned away.", "method": "Builder compared generated HTML/Markdown findings/evidence against tests/fixtures/quality/planted.json, without relying only on dictionary statistics. The added 100x3 shared-absence table now yields co-missing evidence without near-copy findings/exclusions.", "limits": "Builder-authored truth and builder inspection are not Zach/Kate's unseen-table trial. Suspected meanings remain hypotheses, not confirmed defects.", "relations": [{"rel": "supports", "to": "FT-DQ-C1@1"}, {"rel": "observed_on", "to": "code:src.fathom.quality.quality_profile"}, {"rel": "observed_on", "to": "code:tests.test_quality.test_later_builder_holdout_report_evidence"}, {"rel": "supersedes", "to": "FT-DQ-E2@1"}, {"rel": "observed_on", "to": "code:tests.test_quality.test_shared_absence_is_not_near_copy_evidence"}]}
// 2026-11-01 17:56 CT: report order and hidden technical detail (FT-R6@3), sample rows (FT-R9), chart labels (FT-R10). PHI/PII tagging is noted in the decisions file as a future thought, not a requirement.
{"id": "FT-R6", "version": 3, "type": "requirement", "subject": "design", "epistemic": "committed", "text": "The report is readable by a person first. Section order: the per-column dictionary; provenance; the dataset summary (with usefulness and what the data cannot tell); findings grouped by kind (observations, interpretations, hypotheses, suggested actions, open questions), one plain sentence each; relations. Repetitive per-column findings are summarised, with a one-click switch to the full per-item detail (summarised by default; self-contained, no external assets). Technical detail (evidence objects, uncertainty statements, rule versions, hashes and other provenance internals) is hidden by default behind a disclosure or kept to one short line, never a block of its own. Both views derive from the same artifact", "rationale": "Zach 2026-11-01 16:46 CT: slice 1's format is hard to read; he liked the structure of a reference report from an earlier prototype, described here in words (that report is not in this repository)", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "DG-A1@1"}, {"rel": "serves", "to": "DG-RA@1"}, {"rel": "supersedes", "to": "FT-R6@2"}], "anchors": [{"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening.md", "sha256": "2b72ad6573419840163bae048f099578592b43838f3f63e43fd9d8f516bbbab8", "locator": "q:The format is atrocious to read", "for": "rationale"}, {"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening.md", "sha256": "2b72ad6573419840163bae048f099578592b43838f3f63e43fd9d8f516bbbab8", "locator": "q:I liked the format of guidereport.html.", "for": "rationale"}, {"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening-2.md", "sha256": "4f7785545588bc80069709a7faf775df9106ca5cb5b593ef48382c403bb7be5a", "locator": "q:Is it possible to have that summarization piece clickable in the HTML", "for": "rationale"}, {"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening-3.md", "sha256": "e457f180f143231284f7348e1d13653834eacdfa5ed86aed44ff3df2583c22fc", "locator": "q:the report to actually go dictionary, then provenance, then summary, then findings and relations.", "for": "rationale"}, {"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening-3.md", "sha256": "e457f180f143231284f7348e1d13653834eacdfa5ed86aed44ff3df2583c22fc", "locator": "q:we can put that in as a default hidden option or on the same line.", "for": "rationale"}]}
{"id": "FT-R9", "version": 1, "type": "requirement", "subject": "design", "epistemic": "committed", "text": "The report shows sample rows so a reader can see what the data looks like: the first N, the last N and a random N rows (N about 20, configurable; the random sample uses a fixed, recorded seed so output stays deterministic), each as a compact table. Long text is truncated with a visible marker. Per column, the dictionary shows example values, including for text and categorical columns. The rows are copied from the input, so the report notes that it contains raw values", "rationale": "Stated by Zach 2026-11-01 17:56 CT; he used first/last/random samples in earlier work. The exact N is open (\"There's probably a better specific number\")", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "DG-A1@1"}, {"rel": "serves", "to": "P0@2"}], "anchors": [{"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening-3.md", "sha256": "e457f180f143231284f7348e1d13653834eacdfa5ed86aed44ff3df2583c22fc", "locator": "q:I used to have several samples, the first 20, the last 20, random 20 rows", "for": "rationale"}]}
{"id": "FT-R10", "version": 1, "type": "requirement", "subject": "design", "epistemic": "committed", "text": "Every chart has labelled axes (with units or counts stated) and a title saying what is plotted; the missing-cells-by-column chart labels its x axis. Charts are legible without the surrounding text", "rationale": "Stated by Zach 2026-11-01 17:56 CT about the missing-cells-by-column chart; generalised to all charts by claude:aec (the general part is a reading, narrower than nothing, for Zach to correct)", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "DG-A1@1"}], "anchors": [{"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening-3.md", "sha256": "e457f180f143231284f7348e1d13653834eacdfa5ed86aed44ff3df2583c22fc", "locator": "q:Let's put an x-axis label there as well of for the missing cells by column.", "for": "rationale"}]}
// 2026-11-01 17:59 CT: dataset title and description (FT-R11).
{"id": "FT-R11", "version": 1, "type": "requirement", "subject": "design", "epistemic": "committed", "text": "The report opens with the dataset's title and a short description of what it is. Both come from the configuration when supplied (labelled human). Otherwise the title falls back to the file name, and the description is inferred from the column names, types and shape (for example, what kinds of identifiers, amounts, dates, ratings and categories the columns suggest) and is labelled inferred, with its basis shown (DG-RB). The rule-based inference stays a sketch; a richer AI-written description, if ever added, goes in the separate labelled AI layer (DG-A3)", "rationale": "Stated by Zach 2026-11-01 17:59 CT. How the inference is done is a reading by claude:aec, kept rule-based because the AI narrative layer isn't specified (DG-A3)", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "DG-A1@1"}, {"rel": "serves", "to": "DG-RB@1"}], "anchors": [{"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening-4.md", "sha256": "d4266d1dd55f82660276fa6d08aec9a53d1deb33a341514e107db94ff6b4d665", "locator": "q:Also, a title of the data set and a description of what it is", "for": "rationale"}]}
// 2026-11-01 18:00 CT: special types (FT-R12), PDF/PowerPoint exports (FT-R13), no quality score for now (DG-D6).
{"id": "FT-R12", "version": 1, "type": "requirement", "subject": "design", "epistemic": "committed", "text": "Special types are recognised and checked: phone numbers (format validity, placeholders such as 000-000-0000), email addresses (format validity, most common domains, test addresses such as test@test.com), URLs (structure, most common domains) and latitude/longitude (range checks). Recognition is inferred and labelled (DG-RB); checks report counts and examples, never correct values; no network lookups", "rationale": "Zach 2026-11-01 18:00 CT: special types now. The list follows his earlier notes (phone, email, URL, spatial ranges); details are claude:aec's reading", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "DG-RA@1"}, {"rel": "serves", "to": "P0@2"}], "anchors": [{"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening-5.md", "sha256": "923797c79bb411096a52625ba498b0f99bf577dbd59a26fd9b36fbe6f080587c", "locator": "q:Special types now, quality score off for now, and yes to the other export formats.", "for": "rationale"}]}
{"id": "FT-R13", "version": 1, "type": "requirement", "subject": "design", "epistemic": "committed", "text": "Besides self-contained HTML and Markdown, the report exports to PDF and PowerPoint, both derived from the same saved artifact (DG-RE). Export dependencies are optional extras that degrade cleanly when absent (DG-RF); the PDF keeps the summarised view and the sample-row tables; the PowerPoint carries the summary, the key findings and the charts, one topic per slide", "rationale": "Zach 2026-11-01 18:00 CT: yes to PDF and PowerPoint exports. Slide and page contents are claude:aec's reading", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "DG-RE@1"}, {"rel": "serves", "to": "DG-A1@1"}], "anchors": [{"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening-5.md", "sha256": "923797c79bb411096a52625ba498b0f99bf577dbd59a26fd9b36fbe6f080587c", "locator": "q:Special types now, quality score off for now, and yes to the other export formats.", "for": "rationale"}]}
{"id": "DG-D6", "version": 1, "type": "decision", "status": "accepted", "decided_by": "AU-owner", "subject": "design", "text": "No overall data-quality score for now: report the quality measures and findings, but no weighted score or sub-scores until Zach decides otherwise", "anchors": [{"path": "docs/decisions/2026-11-01-evening-5.md", "sha256": "923797c79bb411096a52625ba498b0f99bf577dbd59a26fd9b36fbe6f080587c", "locator": "q:Special types now, quality score off for now, and yes to the other export formats.", "for": "rationale"}]}
// 2026-11-01: advanced univariate detail (FT-R14, provisional), from Zach's 2024 notes and trial-2 misses.
{"id": "FT-R14", "version": 1, "type": "requirement", "subject": "design", "epistemic": "provisional", "text": "Advanced univariate profiles and visuals. Numeric: zeros and negatives (count, share), integer vs non-integer counts, percentiles 1/5/25/33/50/66/75/95/99, skewness and kurtosis with one-line plain interpretations, most and least common values, placeholder patterns; histogram plus a density or box view. Text/categorical: blank variants (empty, whitespace, 'NA', 'none', 'blank', counted as candidate blanks, not silently converted), string length min/max/mean/median, all-upper/all-lower and mixed-casing counts, special-character frequency, most common substrings, Shannon entropy, top-N coverage (dominance), sparse/rare categories; bar chart of top categories. Temporal: parse common non-ISO date formats (labelled inferred, with the format shown), earliest/latest and span, record counts by day of week, month, quarter and year, unusually large gaps, placeholder dates (e.g. 1900-01-01) and implausible dates (after the snapshot date or far in the future), lag-1 autocorrelation of period counts; a counts-over-time chart. Identifier-like columns: repeated values. Quantities: zeros where most values are large (implausible-zero candidates). All charts follow FT-R10", "rationale": "Scope accepted by Zach in DG-D5 (\"more advanced univariate analyses and visuals\"); the detail list is drawn from his December 2024 notes (excerpts in docs/decisions/2024-12-design-notes-excerpts.md) and from fathom-001 trial 2 misses on real data (non-ISO and impossible dates, implausible zeros, repeated identifiers). Provisional until Zach accepts the detail", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "DG-RA@1"}, {"rel": "serves", "to": "P0@2"}], "anchors": [{"path": "docs/decisions/2024-12-design-notes-excerpts.md", "sha256": "69fb08e3b7c58fae052e367b1d9690c993e2c853eaec33fea66a8f29ed84799d", "locator": "q:Skewness and kurtosis (with simple interpretations).", "for": "rationale"}, {"path": "docs/decisions/2024-12-design-notes-excerpts.md", "sha256": "69fb08e3b7c58fae052e367b1d9690c993e2c853eaec33fea66a8f29ed84799d", "locator": "q:Calculate Shannon entropy to measure diversity in categorical values.", "for": "rationale"}, {"path": "docs/decisions/2024-12-design-notes-excerpts.md", "sha256": "69fb08e3b7c58fae052e367b1d9690c993e2c853eaec33fea66a8f29ed84799d", "locator": "q:Visualizations are more important to me to add in.", "for": "rationale"}]}
